# Abstraction

**Abstraction** is the identification of a structure apart from the incidental
details of the things that have it. From permutations and from rotations it
extracts the notion of a group. Its result is a new object of thought, the
structure, which the starting material did not contain as a part. The word is
also used for something else, the abstract of a paper, and the discussion
insists the two are different operations.

| | Abstraction | Writing an abstract |
|---|---|---|
| Input | several concrete structures | one large source |
| Output | the structure they share | a shorter text with the same content |
| What is dropped | whatever is not structure | whatever matters less for the purpose |
| What is new | a concept that applies beyond the inputs | nothing |
| Card | this one | [[summarization]] |

## Description

### Lane one: abstract thinking

The thesis that opened this thread of the discussion was short: simplification,
abstract thinking and generating an abstract summary of a paper are not the
same thing. The reply sorted them into separate operations, each with its own
example.

| Operation | Example |
|---|---|
| Simplification | Point-mass approximation of a physical system |
| Summarization | A shorter abstract of a technical paper |
| Abstraction | Identifying group structure across permutations and rotations |
| Generalization | Extending vector spaces to modules |
| Analogy | Mapping structure between electrical and hydraulic systems |
| Idealization | Frictionless planes or ideal gases |
| Synthesis | New framework integrating established ideas |

Abstraction differs from each neighbour in a specific way.

- **From summarization.** A summary is about the same thing as its source and
  says less. An abstraction is about something else, the structure, and says
  it once for every case that has it.
- **From simplification and idealization.** Those replace a system with an
  easier one that is strictly false of the original: no body is a point mass.
  An abstraction is true of everything it was abstracted from.
- **From generalization.** Abstraction finds the structure; generalization
  widens the range of things a concept or claim covers. The discussion's
  earlier table lists them together, with groups, modules and categories as
  examples, and the later one separates them.
- **From analogy.** Analogy maps one structure onto another. It needs both
  structures identified first, so abstraction comes before it.

In the three-layer organisation proposed in the discussion, abstraction is on
the first layer, among the operations, next to iteration, inversion, induction
and generation. What it produces lives on the second layer: invariance,
equivalence, the structural relationships.

### Lane two: the abstract of a paper

An abstract condenses. It is a product of [[summarization]] and is judged by
whether it keeps what matters for its reader. Nothing in it has to be more
abstract, in the first sense, than the paper it shortens. The shared word is
the whole connection.

The distinction matters for work with language models, which is where the
discussion raised it. Summarization is a task such a model performs routinely.
Being asked for the structure common to several cases is a different request,
and a fluent paragraph that mentions all the cases is not an answer to it. A
system that offers both operations should give them different types: one
returns a shorter text, the other returns a structure together with the
mapping from each case onto it, which can be checked.

### Levels

Abstraction is how levels of description come to exist. A formula is an
abstraction over its instances, a theory over its formulas, a statement about
theories over theories. A [[strange-loop]] needs such levels before it can
cross them, so abstraction is one of its preconditions and not one of its
forms.

## Relations

- It is one [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: instance-of}: concrete structures in, common abstract structure out.
- It is not [[summarization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: one finds a structure, the other shortens a text.
- It is distinct from [[generalization]] {relation: distinguished-from}, with which the first table of the discussion had merged it.
- It comes before [[analogy]] {relation: part-of}: the structures have to be identified before they are mapped.
- It is not [[simplification]] {relation: distinguished-from} or [[idealization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: an abstraction stays true of its cases.
- It produces the levels that [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses} names and that [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses} crosses.
- It is close to [[induction]] {relation: contrasts}: induction infers a rule from cases on evidence, abstraction extracts a structure the cases demonstrably have.
- As an operation of an agent it should be a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: is-a}.
- It bears on the question [[learning-the-formalism]] {relation: answers}, as one of the operations mapped there.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[summarization]].** Condensing a source while keeping what matters for a
  purpose. It is what the word "abstract" means on the first page of a paper,
  and nothing more.
- **[[generalization]].** Extending a concept or claim to a wider domain, as
  vector spaces extend to modules. It works on the reach of a concept, where
  abstraction works on its content.
- **[[analogy]].** A partial mapping between two domains that preserves chosen
  relations. It is abstraction applied twice and then compared.
- **[[object-and-meta-level]].** The distinction between a system and
  statements about it. Each step up is an act of abstraction.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[abstraction]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[abstraction]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[classify-operation]] would have to
tell this operation from summarization, which is the test case for that lens.

